  <div class="section" id="key-findings">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">6</div>
      <h2>Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-icon">♟</div>
      <div>
        <h4>White's First-Move Advantage Is Statistically Real but Skill-Dependent</h4>
        <p>Across all 20,058 games, white wins <strong>52.3% of decisive games</strong> — a meaningful 4.7pp edge. However, this advantage degrades with player strength. At 2000+ Elo, white leads by only 1.1pp while the draw rate doubles to 8%. Coaches should frame white's advantage not as a guarantee but as a marginal initiative that disappears against skilled defenders. Below 1400 Elo, white's structural edge is most actionable (+7.3pp at 1200–1400).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-icon">📊</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Rating Differential Overwhelms Color Advantage Above ±100 Points</h4>
        <p>A 200+ Elo rating gap produces a <strong>78.6% win rate for the stronger player</strong>, regardless of color. Below 50 points the outcome is statistically random (50.4%). Platform analysts: ±50 Elo should be the maximum acceptable gap for "fair" matchmaking. Games with 200+ point gaps are effectively decided before move 1 — they inflate win-loss records and harm both player development and platform satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-icon">📖</div>
      <div>
        <h4>9 of the Top 15 Openings Favor Black — a Critical Opening Anomaly</h4>
        <p>Contrary to classical chess theory, the majority of high-frequency openings in this dataset produce better results for black. The A00 Van't Kruijs (1.e3/1.Nh3) gives black a <strong>27.2pp advantage</strong> — suggesting white is routinely playing passive first moves and ceding the initiative. The B30 Old Sicilian (+20.8pp black) and Bowdler Attack (+15.2pp black) follow. White players at this Elo range would benefit from studying principled opening play; passive systems are a liability, not a safety net.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-icon">⚔️</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Resignation Is the Engine of This Dataset — Checkmate Is the Exception</h4>
        <p>55.6% of games end by resignation, 31.5% by checkmate, 8.4% by timeout. Resigned games are <strong>18% shorter</strong> than mated games (54 vs. 65 turns). This matters for game-length analysis: any "average game" metric is dominated by resigned games. Coaches should note that only 4.5% of games end in drawn positions — this mid-rated pool rarely reaches true endgame theory relevance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-icon">⏱</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Increment Dramatically Changes Game Character — Not Just Length</h4>
        <p>Adding a per-move increment doesn't just prevent timeouts; it reshapes game outcomes. 15+10 produces a <strong>7.8% draw rate vs. 4.0% for 15+0</strong> — nearly double. The 8+0 format's 17.9% timeout rate is the dataset's highest outlier, suggesting that fixed-time-no-increment formats at the 8-minute level create disproportionate time pressure. Platform designers and tournament directors should consider increment-based formats as a draw rate and game quality lever.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-icon">📈</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Higher-Rated Players Play Longer, Draw More, and Resign Less Per Turn</h4>
        <p>Average game length increases monotonically from <strong>48 turns (sub-1000)</strong> to <strong>68 turns (2000+)</strong>, a 41% increase. The draw rate rises from 2.2% to 8.0% over the same range. This pattern reflects accumulating defensive skill: strong players extend games, hold worse positions, and convert drawing resources that lower-rated players miss. Analysts measuring "game quality" by length would see 2000+ games as 42% richer in content than beginner games.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
